1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,750
This video walks through the Modeling tab of Battery Builder, one value at a time.

2
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:13,609
When you press Extract, the app fills the boxes of the Simscape Battery Table-Based block.

3
00:00:13,609 --> 00:00:20,621
That block is an equivalent circuit: an open-circuit voltage source, a series resistance R0,

4
00:00:20,621 --> 00:00:25,575
and two RC pairs, every one of them a table over state of charge,

5
00:00:25,575 --> 00:00:28,243
plus a capacity and a thermal mass.

6
00:00:28,243 --> 00:00:34,264
Every number you will see here was calculated by the app's own extraction code,

7
00:00:34,264 --> 00:00:41,429
on a test file that follows your profile: five CC-CV charges at 0.34 C, and discharges at 0.1,

8
00:00:41,429 --> 00:00:45,239
0.2, 0.5, 1 and 5 C, about seventy hours in total.

9
00:00:45,239 --> 00:00:50,117
The file is synthetic, built from a cell whose values are known,

10
00:00:50,117 --> 00:00:53,471
so we can also check how close the app gets.

11
00:00:55,071 --> 00:00:57,462
Here are the equations of that block.

12
00:00:57,462 --> 00:01:03,342
The terminal voltage is the open-circuit voltage minus the drop across R0 minus the voltage

13
00:01:03,342 --> 00:01:04,634
across each RC pair.

14
00:01:04,634 --> 00:01:10,321
x k is the current through the resistor of pair k; it follows the real current with time

15
00:01:10,321 --> 00:01:11,290
constant tau k.

16
00:01:11,290 --> 00:01:17,364
SOC is coulomb counting, and the temperature is a lumped thermal mass heated by the losses and

17
00:01:17,364 --> 00:01:18,786
cooled by the chamber.

18
00:01:18,786 --> 00:01:24,666
The key idea of the whole tab is on the right: each quantity can only be measured where the

19
00:01:24,666 --> 00:01:26,152
test actually shows it.

20
00:01:26,152 --> 00:01:30,934
R0 shows up where the current steps, the RC pairs where the current stops,

21
00:01:30,934 --> 00:01:33,519
and the open-circuit voltage everywhere,

22
00:01:33,519 --> 00:01:37,137
because the same discharge is repeated at several rates.

23
00:01:38,737 --> 00:01:40,864
This is the whole test.

24
00:01:40,864 --> 00:01:44,838
Top: current in C-rate, positive is charge.

25
00:01:44,838 --> 00:01:46,317
Middle: voltage.

26
00:01:46,317 --> 00:01:50,107
Bottom: the chamber and cell temperature.

27
00:01:50,107 --> 00:01:53,435
The same pattern repeats five times.

28
00:01:53,835 --> 00:01:59,045
A constant-current, constant-voltage charge at 0.34 C, a rest,

29
00:01:59,045 --> 00:02:04,675
a discharge all the way to the 2.75 volt cut-off, and another rest.

30
00:02:04,675 --> 00:02:11,314
The discharges get faster each time: 0.1 C, 0.2 C, 0.5 C, 1 C, and finally 5 C.

31
00:02:11,314 --> 00:02:15,683
That ladder of rates is what makes this a rate test,

32
00:02:15,683 --> 00:02:22,910
and it is what lets the app measure the open-circuit voltage over the whole SOC range.

33
00:02:24,510 --> 00:02:26,249
Now the chamber.

34
00:02:26,649 --> 00:02:32,938
The chamber always starts at 25 °C, and the first charge happens at 25.

35
00:02:33,338 --> 00:02:38,993
Right after the charge finishes, the chamber moves to this file's own temperature,

36
00:02:38,993 --> 00:02:40,096
here 10 degrees,

37
00:02:40,496 --> 00:02:43,420
and holds it through the rest and the discharge.

38
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:47,903
When the discharge is done, it goes back to 25 for the rest,

39
00:02:47,903 --> 00:02:50,556
and the next charge starts at 25 again.

40
00:02:50,556 --> 00:02:55,796
The red line is the cell; it follows the chamber with a lag of a few minutes.

41
00:02:55,796 --> 00:03:01,852
So in every file, the discharges are at the file's temperature and the charges are at 25.

42
00:03:01,852 --> 00:03:08,180
Because of that, the app now takes the test temperature as the chamber temperature during the

43
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:13,964
discharges, not the median of the whole file, which could land on 25 for a cold test.

44
00:03:15,564 --> 00:03:19,379
The resistances and time constants depend on temperature,

45
00:03:19,379 --> 00:03:25,201
so a table for 10 degrees may only use samples where the cell really was at 10 degrees.

46
00:03:25,201 --> 00:03:29,819
The green band is plus or minus 3 kelvin around the test temperature.

47
00:03:29,819 --> 00:03:33,299
The orange dots are the discharge samples inside it.

48
00:03:33,299 --> 00:03:34,905
Two things fall outside.

49
00:03:35,305 --> 00:03:39,914
First, the charges, because they run at 25.

50
00:03:40,314 --> 00:03:46,475
Second, the end of the 5 C discharge, which heats the cell by about 27 kelvin.

51
00:03:46,475 --> 00:03:50,977
In this file that leaves out 6 current steps and 6 rests.

52
00:03:50,977 --> 00:03:58,165
In a 25 degree file nothing is outside except the 5 C end, so the charges are used as well.

53
00:03:59,765 --> 00:04:06,576
The first thing the extraction does is cut the test into phases: charge, discharge and rest,

54
00:04:06,576 --> 00:04:08,574
by the sign of the current.

55
00:04:08,574 --> 00:04:15,162
A charge phase is a CC-CV charge when its current tapered below 35 percent of its maximum

56
00:04:15,162 --> 00:04:19,011
while the voltage stayed flat, within 15 millivolts.

57
00:04:19,011 --> 00:04:20,492
You can see it here:

58
00:04:20,892 --> 00:04:25,585
constant current until the voltage reaches 4.20 volts,

59
00:04:25,985 --> 00:04:29,319
then the voltage is held and the current tapers away.

60
00:04:29,719 --> 00:04:32,751
The end of that taper is called FULL.

61
00:04:32,751 --> 00:04:37,175
It is the reference point for everything that follows.

62
00:04:37,175 --> 00:04:42,992
In this file the app finds 5 charges, 5 discharges and 5 CC-CV charges.

63
00:04:44,592 --> 00:04:45,684
Capacity, AH.

64
00:04:45,684 --> 00:04:52,318
The app adds up the current over time; that is the blue line, net amp-hours in.

65
00:04:52,318 --> 00:04:56,685
It rises during charges and falls during discharges.

66
00:04:57,085 --> 00:05:02,533
The green circles are the FULL points at the end of each CC-CV charge.

67
00:05:02,933 --> 00:05:10,561
The red squares are EMPTY points: the end of a discharge that stopped at the cut-off voltage.

68
00:05:10,561 --> 00:05:16,959
The capacity is simply the drop between a FULL point and the next EMPTY point.

69
00:05:16,959 --> 00:05:20,978
For the 0.1 C discharge that is 2.9771 amp-hours.

70
00:05:20,978 --> 00:05:28,688
As a cross-check, the cycler's own capacity column gives 2.9772, a difference of 0.00 percent.

71
00:05:30,288 --> 00:05:32,389
Why trust that point as empty?

72
00:05:32,389 --> 00:05:34,350
The app checks three things.

73
00:05:34,350 --> 00:05:40,933
The discharge lasted at least five minutes, it ended at the cut-off, and it ended on the knee.

74
00:05:40,933 --> 00:05:45,625
A lithium-ion cell's voltage falls steeply when it is really empty.

75
00:05:46,025 --> 00:05:52,113
Over the middle half of this discharge the voltage falls 315 millivolts per amp-hour;

76
00:05:52,513 --> 00:05:56,067
over the last three percent it falls 3359.

77
00:05:56,067 --> 00:06:03,345
That is 11 times steeper, more than the required three, so this is a true empty point,

78
00:06:03,345 --> 00:06:05,376
not a partial discharge.

79
00:06:06,976 --> 00:06:12,853
The test has 5 full-to-empty discharges, and each gives a different number.

80
00:06:12,853 --> 00:06:18,259
A faster discharge loses more voltage across the internal resistance,

81
00:06:18,259 --> 00:06:25,468
so it reaches the cut-off sooner and delivers fewer amp-hours: here from 2.977 at 0.1 C down

82
00:06:25,468 --> 00:06:26,722
to 2.912 at 5 C.

83
00:06:27,122 --> 00:06:33,825
The slowest discharge is the closest to the charge the cell really stores, so it becomes AH.

84
00:06:33,825 --> 00:06:36,740
The AH How window shows this same table.

85
00:06:38,340 --> 00:06:39,728
State of charge.

86
00:06:39,728 --> 00:06:47,451
With AH known, SOC is coulomb counting: one plus the amp-hours put in since the last FULL

87
00:06:47,451 --> 00:06:49,273
point, divided by AH.

88
00:06:49,673 --> 00:06:56,171
It is reset to exactly one at every FULL point, so errors never build up from cycle to cycle.

89
00:06:56,571 --> 00:07:01,421
Before the first full charge, the same line is run backwards.

90
00:07:01,421 --> 00:07:08,576
It took 2.898 amp-hours to fill the cell the first time, so the file started at SOC 0.026,

91
00:07:08,576 --> 00:07:14,698
almost empty, which matches a test that starts on a cell left at its cut-off.

92
00:07:15,098 --> 00:07:20,536
Notice that after the 5 C discharge, SOC stops at 0.02,

93
00:07:20,536 --> 00:07:26,371
not zero: the cell hit the cut-off with charge still in it.

94
00:07:26,371 --> 00:07:34,875
As a check, the first rest voltage, 3.034 volts, read backwards through the OCV table,

95
00:07:34,875 --> 00:07:36,457
gives SOC 0.029.

96
00:07:36,457 --> 00:07:37,644
That agrees.

97
00:07:39,244 --> 00:07:41,176
R0, the series resistance.

98
00:07:41,176 --> 00:07:43,629
Look at the start of a discharge.

99
00:07:43,629 --> 00:07:49,873
The voltage over an RC pair cannot jump, because its capacitor needs time to charge.

100
00:07:49,873 --> 00:07:56,191
So the instant jump in voltage when the current steps is the series resistance alone.

101
00:07:56,591 --> 00:08:01,284
The last sample before the step is 4.1923 volts,

102
00:08:01,684 --> 00:08:04,647
the first one after it is 4.1881.

103
00:08:04,647 --> 00:08:11,110
That is a change of -4.2 millivolts for a current change of -0.300 amps,

104
00:08:11,110 --> 00:08:14,431
so R0 is 14.00 milliohms at SOC 1.00.

105
00:08:14,431 --> 00:08:21,433
The step only counts if the first sample came within five seconds of the step,

106
00:08:21,433 --> 00:08:29,153
the step is at least 0.08 C, and the cell was within 3 kelvin of the test temperature.

107
00:08:30,753 --> 00:08:33,484
Here is every current step in the test.

108
00:08:33,484 --> 00:08:39,716
The blue circles are the steps that were used; they sit at the two ends of the SOC range,

109
00:08:39,716 --> 00:08:46,227
at the start and the end of each discharge, because that is the only place the current steps.

110
00:08:46,227 --> 00:08:51,059
The red crosses were left out: the steps at the start of each charge,

111
00:08:51,059 --> 00:08:56,940
which happen at 25 degrees, and the end of the 5 C discharge, where the cell is hot.

112
00:08:56,940 --> 00:09:02,612
Each SOC breakpoint takes the median of the steps nearest it; those are marked M.

113
00:09:02,612 --> 00:09:07,793
In the middle of the range there are no steps, so those entries, marked S,

114
00:09:07,793 --> 00:09:11,574
come from the multi-rate fit you will see in a moment.

115
00:09:11,574 --> 00:09:13,955
The dotted line is the known cell.

116
00:09:13,955 --> 00:09:15,705
8 steps used, 6 left out.

117
00:09:17,305 --> 00:09:19,985
The RC pairs come from the rests.

118
00:09:19,985 --> 00:09:24,451
When the current stops, the R0 drop disappears at once,

119
00:09:24,451 --> 00:09:28,267
and what is left is the RC voltages dying away.

120
00:09:28,267 --> 00:09:33,707
That relaxation is a sum of exponentials, one per pair, settling to

121
00:09:34,107 --> 00:09:40,192
V infinity, the fully relaxed voltage, which is also an open-circuit voltage point.

122
00:09:40,192 --> 00:09:46,643
The branch currents at the moment the current stopped are computed from the real current

123
00:09:46,643 --> 00:09:49,209
history, here -3.00 and -3.00 amps.

124
00:09:49,209 --> 00:09:55,513
For given time constants, the resistances are a linear least-squares problem; the time

125
00:09:55,513 --> 00:09:59,472
constants are found by a grid search and then refined.

126
00:09:59,872 --> 00:10:08,570
This rest, after a discharge at SOC 0.01, gives a fast pair with τ1 of 11.2 seconds

127
00:10:08,970 --> 00:10:13,498
and a slow pair with τ2 of 270 seconds.

128
00:10:13,498 --> 00:10:21,972
R1 is 7.64 and R2 is 7.99 milliohms, with a fit error of 0.54 millivolts.

129
00:10:23,572 --> 00:10:29,075
Next is the heart of the tab, the multi-rate fit, but it needs one preparation.

130
00:10:29,075 --> 00:10:33,951
Right after a discharge starts, the RC voltages are still building up.

131
00:10:33,951 --> 00:10:39,663
A sample is only used once what is left of every transient is below one millivolt,

132
00:10:39,663 --> 00:10:42,588
using the R and tau values from the rests.

133
00:10:42,988 --> 00:10:47,124
That wait is 2, 4, 6, 7 minutes for the 0.1, 0.2, 0.5,

134
00:10:47,124 --> 00:10:51,642
1 C discharges; the faint parts of each curve are not used.

135
00:10:51,642 --> 00:10:56,160
This is also where the 25 degree problem you saw came from.

136
00:10:56,160 --> 00:11:03,359
In a 25 degree file the rests after the CC-CV charges are not too warm, so they were included.

137
00:11:03,359 --> 00:11:10,327
But a rest after a constant-voltage taper starts with almost no current in the RC branches,

138
00:11:10,327 --> 00:11:16,301
so its R and tau are poorly defined, and on real data they can come out large.

139
00:11:16,301 --> 00:11:21,738
That made the wait longer than the 0.5 C and 1 C discharges themselves,

140
00:11:21,738 --> 00:11:27,252
those rates dropped out, and the OCV could not cover the full SOC range.

141
00:11:27,252 --> 00:11:33,761
In the other files those rests were left out because the chamber had already changed,

142
00:11:33,761 --> 00:11:36,518
which is why only 25 degrees failed.

143
00:11:36,518 --> 00:11:43,027
The wait now uses only rests that follow a discharge, and if a rate is still dropped,

144
00:11:43,027 --> 00:11:46,167
the OCV note tells you which one and why.

145
00:11:47,767 --> 00:11:49,718
Here is the multi-rate fit.

146
00:11:49,718 --> 00:11:55,644
Once the RC pairs have settled, every branch current equals the discharge current,

147
00:11:55,644 --> 00:12:01,642
and the block's equation becomes a straight line in current: the voltage equals the

148
00:12:01,642 --> 00:12:07,713
open-circuit voltage minus the current times R_dc, where R_dc is R0 plus R1 plus R2.

149
00:12:07,713 --> 00:12:11,615
Every discharge starts from FULL, so its SOC is exact,

150
00:12:11,615 --> 00:12:15,373
and all the discharges can be drawn on one SOC axis.

151
00:12:15,373 --> 00:12:18,770
The slower the discharge, the higher its curve.

152
00:12:18,770 --> 00:12:25,635
The 5 C discharge is left out: it heats the cell far outside the window and stops being linear.

153
00:12:26,035 --> 00:12:28,999
Now pick one SOC, say 0.5.

154
00:12:28,999 --> 00:12:32,874
The four rates give four voltages.

155
00:12:34,474 --> 00:12:39,192
Plot those four voltages against their current and they fall on a straight line.

156
00:12:39,592 --> 00:12:42,165
Extend the line back to zero current,

157
00:12:42,165 --> 00:12:48,631
and the intercept is the voltage the cell would have with no current at all: the open-circuit

158
00:12:48,631 --> 00:12:51,690
voltage on the discharge side, 3.6239 volts.

159
00:12:52,090 --> 00:12:57,204
The slope is the total D C resistance, 23.16 milliohms.

160
00:12:57,204 --> 00:13:04,550
The points sit 0.61 millivolts from the line, so the straight-line model holds.

161
00:13:04,550 --> 00:13:09,851
The known cell has 3.6249 volts and 24.96 milliohms here.

162
00:13:09,851 --> 00:13:14,035
This is repeated at every one percent of SOC.

163
00:13:15,635 --> 00:13:22,514
Do that at every percent and you get two whole curves: the intercepts give the discharge-side

164
00:13:22,514 --> 00:13:26,878
open-circuit voltage, and the slopes give R_dc against SOC.

165
00:13:26,878 --> 00:13:30,799
The dotted line is the known cell's total resistance.

166
00:13:30,799 --> 00:13:33,166
The fit covers SOC 0.01 to 0.98.

167
00:13:33,166 --> 00:13:40,045
It cannot reach the very top, because the first minutes of every discharge are never settled,

168
00:13:40,045 --> 00:13:42,412
or the very bottom, at the knee.

169
00:13:42,412 --> 00:13:44,705
Those ends come from the rests.

170
00:13:46,305 --> 00:13:50,028
Two more pieces finish the open-circuit voltage.

171
00:13:50,028 --> 00:13:51,812
First, the charge side.

172
00:13:51,812 --> 00:13:58,559
A cell rests slightly higher after a charge than after a discharge; that is hysteresis.

173
00:13:58,559 --> 00:14:05,462
In the 25 degree file, on the left, the 0.34 C charges are inside the temperature window,

174
00:14:05,462 --> 00:14:10,736
so the app corrects them by i times R_dc and averages the two sides.

175
00:14:10,736 --> 00:14:13,606
Half the gap is 0.5 millivolts there.

176
00:14:13,606 --> 00:14:19,190
In a 10 degree file the charges ran at 25 degrees, so they are not used,

177
00:14:19,190 --> 00:14:22,447
and the table is the discharge side alone.

178
00:14:22,447 --> 00:14:23,766
Second, the ends.

179
00:14:23,766 --> 00:14:30,436
The rest after each discharge to cut-off relaxes to the SOC zero open-circuit voltage,

180
00:14:30,436 --> 00:14:36,020
the red circles at the bottom, and a smooth curve joins them to the fit.

181
00:14:36,020 --> 00:14:42,768
On the right is the final V0 table for this file, with its flags: M measured, I joined,

182
00:14:42,768 --> 00:14:43,621
E extended.

183
00:14:43,621 --> 00:14:48,275
Against the known cell, the largest error is 2.8 millivolts.

184
00:14:49,875 --> 00:14:51,897
Now the resistance tables.

185
00:14:51,897 --> 00:14:58,044
Each bar is one SOC breakpoint: R0 in orange, R1 in blue, R2 in green, stacked.

186
00:14:58,044 --> 00:15:01,701
The black line is R_dc from the multi-rate fit.

187
00:15:01,701 --> 00:15:08,780
At the two ends, marked M, all three were measured directly, at the steps and in the rests.

188
00:15:08,780 --> 00:15:15,783
In between, the current never steps or stops, so R0, R1 and R2 cannot be told apart there.

189
00:15:15,783 --> 00:15:18,117
But their sum can be measured.

190
00:15:18,117 --> 00:15:22,162
So the app interpolates them from the measured ends,

191
00:15:22,162 --> 00:15:28,387
then multiplies all three by one common factor so their sum equals R_dc exactly.

192
00:15:28,387 --> 00:15:30,254
Those are the S entries.

193
00:15:30,254 --> 00:15:37,412
The total resistance a constant current feels is always measured; only the split between the

194
00:15:37,412 --> 00:15:39,123
three is interpolated.

195
00:15:39,123 --> 00:15:42,235
The time constants are interpolated too.

196
00:15:43,835 --> 00:15:45,077
The thermal values.

197
00:15:45,077 --> 00:15:51,220
The cell is treated as one lump: its heat capacity times the rate of temperature change equals

198
00:15:51,220 --> 00:15:54,749
the heat generated minus what convects to the chamber.

199
00:15:54,749 --> 00:16:00,761
The heat comes from the measured voltage: current times the gap between the terminal voltage

200
00:16:00,761 --> 00:16:05,662
and the open-circuit voltage, minus the energy stored in the RC capacitors.

201
00:16:05,662 --> 00:16:07,100
The top plot shows it;

202
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:10,984
by far the biggest spike is the 5 C discharge.

203
00:16:10,984 --> 00:16:13,938
The ambient is the real chamber column,

204
00:16:13,938 --> 00:16:18,785
including its steps between 25 degrees and the test temperature,

205
00:16:18,785 --> 00:16:22,345
so the chamber schedule is fully accounted for.

206
00:16:22,345 --> 00:16:25,526
The two unknowns, m·cp and h·A, are fitted

207
00:16:25,926 --> 00:16:32,493
so that the simulated temperature, in blue, follows the measured one, in red.

208
00:16:32,493 --> 00:16:39,571
Result: a thermal mass of 45.3 joules per kelvin and h·A of 0.081 watts per kelvin,

209
00:16:39,571 --> 00:16:45,284
against 45 and 0.08 for the known cell, with 0.06 kelvin rms error.

210
00:16:45,284 --> 00:16:53,215
A sanity check for real cells: m·cp should be close to the cell mass in grams times about one

211
00:16:53,215 --> 00:16:55,091
joule per gram kelvin.

212
00:16:56,691 --> 00:16:57,968
Three small ones.

213
00:16:57,968 --> 00:17:02,929
The voltage range is just the lowest and highest voltage measured.

214
00:17:02,929 --> 00:17:08,115
The initial cell temperature is the first cell reading, 25.0 degrees,

215
00:17:08,115 --> 00:17:11,121
because the chamber always starts at 25.

216
00:17:11,121 --> 00:17:14,353
That is the right start for the simulation,

217
00:17:14,353 --> 00:17:21,417
because batt_ambient from the load profile tab carries the real chamber column, steps and all,

218
00:17:21,417 --> 00:17:22,921
to the thermal port.

219
00:17:22,921 --> 00:17:28,257
And here is everything together, with the workspace name of each value.

220
00:17:28,257 --> 00:17:34,119
Capacity 2.9771 amp-hours, starting SOC 0.026, the open-circuit voltage table,

221
00:17:34,119 --> 00:17:38,553
the resistances and time constants, and the thermal values.

222
00:17:38,553 --> 00:17:44,867
Every one of them came from the charge and discharge cycles exactly as you just saw.

223
00:17:46,467 --> 00:17:50,571
To finish, here is where each value lives in your test.

224
00:17:50,971 --> 00:17:55,764
R0 at the current steps, at the start and end of each discharge.

225
00:17:56,164 --> 00:18:02,168
The RC pairs, and the two ends of the open-circuit voltage curve, in the rests.

226
00:18:02,568 --> 00:18:06,266
The capacity from the slowest full-to-empty discharge.

227
00:18:06,666 --> 00:18:11,700
The open-circuit voltage and the total resistance from all the discharge rates together.

228
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:16,400
And the thermal values from the self-heating of the 5 C discharge.

229
00:18:16,400 --> 00:18:22,330
In the app, press Extract, and every green box has a How button that walks through the same

230
00:18:22,330 --> 00:18:25,067
steps with the numbers from your own file.

231
00:18:25,067 --> 00:18:29,954
And the 25 degree files should now produce an open-circuit voltage as well.

232
00:18:29,954 --> 00:18:35,949
If one still does not, the note on the OCV box will say which discharge rate was dropped and

233
00:18:35,949 --> 00:18:36,210
why.
